cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or the released result records, please cite the paper."
title: >-
  Self-supervised pre-training and sharpness-aware minimisation interact under
  distribution shift in histopathology, and the interaction sets the condition
  for benefit
# NOTE: the manuscript author list is still a placeholder (see paper/main_journal.tex).
# Fill in real authors here and there together before publishing this repository;
# CFF requires at least one author, so this entry is intentionally an obvious stub.
authors:
  - name: "[Author list pending -- do not cite as-is]"
type: software
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/ORG/REPO"
abstract: >-
  Code, per-run result records and manuscript source for a controlled test of
  self-supervised pre-training (SSL) and sharpness-aware minimisation (SAM) on
  two cross-site histopathology benchmarks. The SSL x SAM interaction term is
  positive on every evaluation under distribution shift and absent
  in-distribution; net benefit over a supervised baseline requires the
  interaction to exceed the summed cost of each mechanism applied alone.
  Also includes five patient-disjoint leave-one-hospital-out splits of
  Camelyon17 and a model-free quantification of site shift across them, which
  shows the shift is graded (stain distance varies 1.65-fold across folds while
  domain-classifier separability sits near ceiling) and that a substantial part
  of apparent site separability is patient-level rather than institutional.
keywords:
  - distribution shift
  - self-supervised learning
  - sharpness-aware minimization
  - computational pathology
  - external validation
  - domain generalization
  - multi-site validation
